Setting Up Git & GitHub
Aim: To configure Git and connect a local repository to GitHub.
Procedure: Check Git, configure your identity, create a local repository, commit README.md, and push to GitHub.
Commands to copy into Git Bash
git --version          
git config --global user.name "Ganavi"
git config --global user.email "YOUR_EMAIL"
mkdir git-lab-demo
cd git-lab-demo
git init
echo "# Git Lab Demo" > README.md
git add README.md
git commit -m "First commit"
git branch -M main
git remote add origin https://github.com/ganavip73-arch/git-lab-demo.git
git push -u origin main
Expected output
git version 2.x.x
Initialized empty Git repository
[main (root-commit) abc1234] First commit
1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
[new branch] main -> main
branch 'main' set up to track 'origin/main'.
Result: Git was configured and the local repository was connected to GitHub

Basic Git Workflow (Add, Commit, Push)
Aim: To create and execute a Python program and upload it to GitHub.
Procedure: Create hello.py, run it, check the status, stage the file, commit it, and push the commit.
Commands to copy into Git Bash
echo "print('Hello Git')" > hello.py
python hello.py
git status
git add hello.py
git commit -m "Add hello Python program"
git push origin main
Expected output
Hello Git
[main abc1234] Add hello Python program
1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
To https://github.com/ganavip73-arch/git-lab-demo.git
   abc1234..def5678  main -> main
Result: The Python program was executed, committed, and pushed to GitHub.

Creating and Managing Branches
Aim: To create and manage a feature branch.
Procedure: Switch to main, create feature-branch, create and run feature.py, commit the file, push the branch, and list branches.
Commands to copy into Git Bash
git switch main
git switch -c feature-branch
echo "print('Hello from Feature Branch')" > feature.py
python feature.py
git add feature.py
git commit -m "Add feature branch Python program"
git push -u origin feature-branch
git branch
Expected output
Switched to a new branch 'feature-branch'
Hello from Feature Branch
[feature-branch abc1234] Add feature branch Python program
1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
* feature-branch
  main
Result: A feature branch was created, and the Python program was committed and pushed.


Collaborating with Pull Requests
Aim: To merge changes from a feature branch into main using a GitHub pull request.
Procedure: Push the feature branch, create a pull request on GitHub from feature-branch into main, merge it, and pull the updated main branch locally.
Commands to copy into Git Bash
git switch feature-branch
git push -u origin feature-branch
 
# On GitHub: Pull requests > New pull request
# Base: main; Compare: feature-branch
# Create pull request > Merge pull request > Confirm merge
 
git switch main
git pull origin main
git status
git log --oneline -5
Expected output
Updating abc1234..def5678
Fast-forward
 feature.py | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)
On branch main
Your branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.
nothing to commit, working tree clean
Result: The feature branch changes were merged into main using a pull request.


Stashing Changes
Aim: To temporarily save and restore uncommitted changes.
Procedure: Create and commit a baseline file, append a second line, stash the change, inspect the stash, then restore it.
Commands to copy into Git Bash
git switch main
echo "print('Experiment 5 - Original Version')" > stash_demo.py
git add stash_demo.py
git commit -m "Add stash demo program"
git push origin main
 
echo "print('This change is temporarily saved')" >> stash_demo.py
git stash push -m "Experiment 5 changes"
git stash list
 
git stash pop
cat stash_demo.py
git status
Expected output
Saved working directory and index state
On main: Experiment 5 changes
stash@{0}: On main: Experiment 5 changes
Dropped refs/stash@{0}
print('Experiment 5 - Original Version')
print('This change is temporarily saved')
Changes not staged for commit:
    modified: stash_demo.py
Result: The uncommitted changes were stashed and restored successfully.


Cloning a Remote Repository
Aim: To copy a remote GitHub repository to a local computer.
Procedure: Move to the home directory, clone the GitHub repository into a new folder, enter it, and inspect status, files, and remote URLs.
Commands to copy into Git Bash
cd ~
git clone https://github.com/ganavip73-arch/git-lab-demo.git git-lab-clone
cd git-lab-clone
git status
ls
git remote -v
